---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout title="Founder Story — Built by a Senior Living Chef" description="Why ShorelineOps was created inside a real senior living kitchen dealing with dysphagia textures, allergen risks, and food distributor ordering.">
  <section class="py-20 bg-gradient-to-b from-slate-900/80 to-slate-950">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="text-center mb-16">
        <div class="inline-block px-3 py-1 bg-teal-950/80 border border-teal-800/60 rounded-full text-teal-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">
          Practitioner-Led Healthcare Software
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-6xl font-display font-black text-white tracking-tight mb-6">
          Why I Built ShorelineOps Inside a Real Senior Living Kitchen.
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-300 italic">
          "Nobody selling software to senior living has cooked a pureed dinner service for 80 residents with 14 different allergy restrictions."
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>
          If you’ve ever run a senior living dietary department, you know the daily reality:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li>Nursing calls down at 4:30 PM with three new room moves, a new diabetic diet order, and a resident transitioning to Pureed texture.</li>
          <li>Your morning cook is trying to read handwriting on a greasy dry-erase board while balancing 4 pots on the range.</li>
          <li>You’re trying to build your weekly order for <strong>Dennis Food Service</strong> using clipboards and guesswork, hoping you don't run out of thickener before Friday.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-white pt-6">The Legacy Software Failure</h2>
        <p>
          When I looked at what the software industry offered, I found two extremes:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
          <li><strong>Enterprise Monopolies ($1,200+/month)</strong>: Bulky software designed for 500-bed hospital chains, bloated with features no 60-bed assisted living facility will ever touch, and locked into rigid distributor contracts.</li>
          <li><strong>Binders and Clipboards</strong>: What 80% of independent facilities actually use because the alternatives are too expensive or difficult to train staff on.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-white pt-6">The ShorelineOps Philosophy</h2>
        <p>
          I built <strong>ShorelineOps</strong> to be the tool I wished I had in the kitchen every morning:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Big, touch-friendly tablet buttons</strong> that line cooks can tap with gloved hands.</li>
          <li><strong>Bright red allergy banners</strong> that make it impossible to serve the wrong food to an allergic resident.</li>
          <li><strong>Suggested Dennis Food Service ordering</strong> that calculates <code>Par Level - On Hand</code> automatically so you never over-order or run short.</li>
          <li><strong>Open-source and affordable</strong> so small and medium operators aren't priced out of clinical safety.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl bg-blue-950/40 border border-blue-800/60 my-10 text-center">
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-2">Let’s Talk Kitchen Operations</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm max-w-xl mx-auto mb-6">
            Whether you’re a Dietary Manager in Maine or an Executive Director looking to tighten food spend, I’d love to show you how we run ShorelineOps.
          </p>
          <a href="http://localhost:3000" class="inline-flex px-8 py-3 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-500 text-white font-bold rounded-xl text-sm transition-all shadow-lg shadow-blue-600/30">
            Test the Live Demo Sandbox
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
